WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.300
APIs and distributed applications often use token-based sessions or token-based authentication.

2
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:14.200
A bearer token at a high level grants access to whoever presents it within its validity and scope.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.820
Access tokens may be short lived and used for API calls.

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.360
Refresh tokens may be used to obtain new access tokens.

5
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:28.900
API keys and service tokens may represent clients, integrations, or workloads rather than human users.

6
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.740
Token validation must be precise.

7
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:44.560
Depending on the design, the application may need to verify validity, issuer, audience, expiration, signature or integrity, token type, intended use, scopes, and claims.

8
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:47.220
A token being present is not enough.

9
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.040
A token meant for one service should not automatically be accepted by another.

10
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.700
A token with identity information should not be treated as permission for every action.

11
00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.580
Token storage decisions depend on client type and risk.

12
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:10.680
A browser application, server-rendered Python app, backend service, CLI tool, mobile client, and background worker have different storage constraints.

13
00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:17.180
Long-lived tokens require careful protection, rotation, revocation, and monitoring.

14
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.480
Refresh tokens deserve particular care because they can extend access over time.

15
00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:27.940
Logs, error messages, telemetry, and support tools should not expose tokens.

16
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:34.620
API authentication and browser session management are related but not interchangeable.

17
00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:37.520
Cookies may be well suited to some browser flows.

18
00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.220
Bearer tokens may be appropriate for API and service calls.

19
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:45.960
Service-to-service sessions may use workload identity or service accounts.

20
00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:50.220
In every case, token presence does not replace authorization checks.

21
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:58.800
After the caller is authenticated, the application still needs to verify whether that caller may perform the requested action on the requested resource.
